kernelbase: Clear the caller's buffer in GetProcessMitigationPolicy.

GetProcessMitigationPolicy is a stub that returns TRUE and leaves the
caller's buffer as it was, so a caller that trusts the result reads
whatever was left on its stack.

Firefox does this when it starts. mozglue's IsWin32kLockedDown() asks for
ProcessSystemCallDisablePolicy and keeps DisallowWin32kSystemCalls for the
rest of the process's life. When the leftover bit is set, the main process
believes that win32k system calls are locked down: its app shell creates
no message window and does not observe the main thread's events, so the
main thread never reads Windows messages again and the browser window is
"Not Responding" from the start. Whether it happens depends on the stack,
so it comes and goes from one start to the next. In a hung Firefox 158 the
cached flag was set in the main process and clear in its child processes.

Wine enforces none of these policies, so report every flag as cleared.

Status: not for WineHQ as it stands. This patch was written with an LLM,
and WineHQ does not accept LLM-generated code. WineHQ bug 59529, reported
in March 2026, describes the same stub breaking an embedded Chromium
browser (CefSharp): https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=59529
---
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
@@ -933,7 +933,10 @@
 BOOL WINAPI /* DECLSPEC_HOTPATCH */ GetProcessMitigationPolicy( HANDLE process, PROCESS_MITIGATION_POLICY policy,
                                                           void *buffer, SIZE_T length )
 {
-    FIXME( "(%p, %u, %p, %Iu): stub\n", process, policy, buffer, length );
+    FIXME( "(%p, %u, %p, %Iu): semi-stub\n", process, policy, buffer, length );
+
+    /* No mitigation policy is enforced, so report every flag as cleared. */
+    if (buffer) memset( buffer, 0, length );
     return TRUE;
 }
 
